 To find the matrix that represents a rotation about the origin by an angle of $45^\circ$ clockwise, we need to understand the rotation matrix. The general form for a rotation matrix is:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]
For a rotation clockwise by an angle of $45^\circ$, $\theta = -45^\circ$. Substituting $\theta = -45^\circ$ into the rotation matrix formula, we get:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(-45^\circ) & -\sin(-45^\circ) \\
\sin(-45^\circ) & \cos(-45^\circ)
\end{pmatrix}
\]
We know that $\cos(-45^\circ) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ and $\sin(-45^\circ) = -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$. Thus, the matrix becomes:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
